---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import DocsShell from '../../components/DocsShell.astro';
import { jovianMoonCount } from '../../lib/siteCounts';
---
<BaseLayout title="Rendering docs">
  <DocsShell
    title="Rendering adapts physics for human eyes."
    eyebrow="raylib boundary"
    lede="The renderer converts meters into readable 3D space and never changes the simulation state it receives."
  >
    <section class="doc-panel">
      <h2>Boundary files</h2>
      <ul class="check-list">
        <li><code>src/render/renderer.c</code> owns raylib drawing, render-scale conversion, body colors, the adaptive grid, trails and the draw order.</li>
        <li><code>src/render/render_resources.c</code> owns the lighting shader pair (GLSL 330 on desktop, GLSL 100 for WebGL), sphere and ring meshes, and texture upload.</li>
        <li><code>src/render/scene_style.c</code> is raylib-free presentation math with its own tests: the texture inventory, atmospheres, IAU spin orientation, mesh generation, and the grid, trail and glow fades.</li>
        <li><code>src/render/image_decode.c</code> wraps a pinned, vendored <code>stb_image.h</code>, because raylib ships with JPEG decoding disabled.</li>
        <li><code>src/app/body_trails.c</code> records synchronized SI-unit trail history for non-star bodies and moving stars. Only fixed stars omit history.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="doc-panel">
      <h2>Scale modes</h2>
      <p>Parent-aware visual separation also applies to the encounter probe, so illustrative Earth cannot hide it. Its parent-relative SI state stays unchanged. Real-scale mode retains physical positions and the explicitly nonphysical unknown-radius wire marker.</p>
      <p>Illustrative mode keeps planets readable, renders Vesta as a distinct smaller asteroid, and expands close moon separation only as a visual transform. Real-scale mode uses the same physical scale for positions and radii, so planets and asteroids can become nearly invisible.</p>
      <p>Unknown radii are the explicit exception: wire markers in either view are nonphysical symbols, with Unknown shown in the inspector. Individual-body framing lets you inspect a Jovian moon without framing Jupiter and its entire {jovianMoonCount}-moon family.</p>
      <p>Saturn's textured rings use NASA's ring-system extent and lie in Saturn's equatorial plane from its IAU pole. They affect drawing and camera framing only; gravity and the inspector retain Saturn's JPL mean radius.</p>
      <p>Both modes use the same simulation source. The difference is presentation policy at the raylib boundary.</p>
      <p>Camera-relative subtraction takes place in double precision before conversion to raylib floats. This preserves local detail at large heliocentric distances.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="doc-panel">
      <h2>Lighting, textures and spin</h2>
      <p>Bodies are lit by the rendered Sun with Lambert's cosine law, computed in linear light (texture values are sRGB-encoded, so the shader decodes them, lights them and re-encodes for the display), plus a small ambient term so night sides keep their shape. Earth shows city lights on its night side, a cloud layer and a sun glint on open water. Bodies with air get a glowing limb: an additive shell slightly above the surface, brightest where sight lines graze the thickest atmosphere. Saturn casts its shadow across its rings and the rings cast banded shadows on Saturn, both computed analytically from the Sun's direction. The Sun is emissive with limb darkening and an additive halo. Lessons without a star light their bodies from the camera, and framing a body turns the camera to its sunlit side.</p>
      <p>The Sun, the eight planets, the Moon, Saturn's rings and the Milky Way backdrop wear real maps from Solar System Scope (CC BY 4.0). Moons, Vesta and other bodies without a source-backed map stay honest lit colours rather than borrowed art. The browser fetches the maps after the first frame, so the scene starts at once and sharpens as each arrives; a missing map leaves its body lit in colour and never stops the simulation.</p>
      <p>Labels name the Sun, the planets and the selected body; moons with a known size are named once their family is zoomed in. Labels behind a nearer body are hidden and overlapping ones give way by importance, so a crowded inner system stays readable. They use the site's JetBrains Mono (SIL OFL), embedded in the build so they appear from the first frame; <kbd>H</kbd> or View options → Labels hides them.</p>
      <p>Each textured body turns about its real axis: pole and prime-meridian angle come from the IAU Working Group on Cartographic Coordinates and Rotational Elements (2015 report), converted from the ICRF through the J2000 ecliptic into the simulation's axes. Uranus lies on its side, Venus spins backwards, and Earth's Greenwich meridian matches its J2000 sidereal angle. Spin time counts TDB days from J2000 for the synthetic scene and from JD 2461200.5 for catalog experiments. The synthetic perihelion scene does not place planets at their J2000 positions, so which longitude faces the Sun (and how Saturn's rings tip toward it) is illustrative there; catalog experiments use real positions at their epoch.</p>
      <p>All of this is presentation. Lighting, maps, spin, glow and fades read SI state and never write it: CSV exports and inspector values are identical with or without textures. To stay smooth on phones, bodies behind the camera are skipped, bodies smaller than a pixel are drawn as small dots, and each trail is drawn only as finely as its on-screen size needs (about one point every two pixels, never fewer than 24), so a distant moon family's trails cost a few points instead of thousands.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="doc-panel">
      <h2>Reference grid and trails</h2>
      <p>Absolute history shows motion in the simulation frame. Parent-relative history subtracts synchronized historical parent positions and anchors the result at today's parent position. Neither view changes stored SI history. Optional green velocity and orange acceleration markers have physical directions and explicitly illustrative lengths.</p>
      <p>The ground grid sits just below the orbital plane and adapts to zoom like a CAD grid: minor and major lines fall on powers of ten (one render unit is 0.1 AU), the minor lines cross-fade into the next decade as you zoom out, and every line fades with distance from the camera target, so it never crowds into moiré. It writes no depth, so bodies always draw over it; <kbd>G</kbd> (or View options → Grid) hides it. Trails fade with age: recent motion is bright, older history dims toward a floor rather than vanishing. They are drawn after the planets with additive blending and stop at each body's surface, so they glow over a planet instead of scratching it.</p>
      <p>Trail history starts at a 300-second sampling cadence, rounded up to whole configured ticks for lessons. When its 1,025-point budget fills, both historical spacing and future cadence double together, retaining uniform full-run coverage and a current endpoint. Parent and moon histories keep matching sample times, including historical Sun positions in the moving-Sun barycentric lesson.</p>
      <p>Longer runs have coarser history: fine satellite loops eventually lose detail. Trails show recorded motion rather than complete predicted orbital ellipses. The Astro runtime reports the historical spacing outside the canvas.</p>
    </section>
  </DocsShell>
</BaseLayout>
